   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# portlog\n\nKnow what held a port—even after the process exits.\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time, so you can answer “what was on port 3000 an hour ago?”\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux.\n\n## Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns the recorder in the background. Stop it with:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## Look up a port\n\nSee every process that held port 3000, newest first:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nLimit the history to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n## Free a port\n\nStop the process holding port 3000 now:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nportlog asks for confirmation first. Add `-y` to skip it.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe recorder polls `lsof` every two seconds on macOS and reads `/proc/net/tcp` on Linux. History stays in `~/.portlog/` for seven days.\n\nCommand-line arguments are never recorded because they can hold secrets.\n\n## About\n\nWritten in Go. MIT licensed.\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What does portlog do?\n(b) What do you run first after installing it?\n(c) Can it show a process that already exited?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (c) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
